5fd07856a21f4b3d2104f4a84040d0186dfac8ae36.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-325922/field-1" "q-325922/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325922"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection/80402b8c67e08f25f6556fe0c2794168a4e6585522c9d73e8ddf0d180efdbab5" :answer/id #uuid "b135a810-260e-5ad9-b396-974ebc5a2415" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection/21a0fff2baaadc90f73afc52152ce0a0224aebdfab845c34172527e00b8a3a04" :answer/id #uuid "22c9d02d-8d94-5152-9fb1-c7e67d44d226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection/f84f89a693d8d7bfc5f943e72d6e195f685cae8f2b290b7548f59c5d2eca497a" :answer/id #uuid "8f15f4f6-ed7b-50f2-8eba-94c77cc30ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection/19ab301adc702903b20db32185d1c91895c6931075b3191f6a48c97971eda8ba" :answer/id #uuid "09cc1b11-c989-5980-918f-48988fe5a14e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection/bed379cbdb515df8795f1108d6c3c2dd5e88cf34ea67558215c697f7a5d471e9" :answer/id #uuid "bab9069e-4c6a-500c-a545-4252f130c0f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "db5aa961-bbd5-5c70-8665-0cfabeaa497c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106521/selection/f84f89a693d8d7bfc5f943e72d6e195f685cae8f2b290b7548f59c5d2eca497a" :answer-field/choices ["q-106521/selection/80402b8c67e08f25f6556fe0c2794168a4e6585522c9d73e8ddf0d180efdbab5" "q-106521/selection/21a0fff2baaadc90f73afc52152ce0a0224aebdfab845c34172527e00b8a3a04" "q-106521/selection/f84f89a693d8d7bfc5f943e72d6e195f685cae8f2b290b7548f59c5d2eca497a" "q-106521/selection/19ab301adc702903b20db32185d1c91895c6931075b3191f6a48c97971eda8ba" "q-106521/selection/bed379cbdb515df8795f1108d6c3c2dd5e88cf34ea67558215c697f7a5d471e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106521" :question/id #uuid "a5eb140c-ede8-53b2-a8e6-2cb164c906ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106521" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077122/assets/2fe325dcc61b0cec5483791e00842ca975b5d712ffed93cf48eacc52a6cd8daf.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106521/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106521"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7987"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection/51360456424422c7319ad71215d3cd2a01be2d133af9b58170a8436c1ffbdb7c" :answer/id #uuid "a04e533a-811c-5256-839d-a6c83c5abf65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection/3c3a2422729e8f6e4970d71ae012fd4cb1e2a604de9d671c1687bee806aa3a17" :answer/id #uuid "a6fb6a4f-c0d7-5522-b257-07d03e6b798f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection/d6390209006219fa20a1dcce44e45fad1af081f229774a713e8b61b52cdcb868" :answer/id #uuid "be18dd9b-7e9d-5a40-bc4b-8397556779d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection/cb3b7e94a0466971bf58267087e6595d91f5f4d83c446828ecbb1a3af7d0640e" :answer/id #uuid "4b7ba4ef-58c2-5764-9b02-0be553f2b8f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection/de3475c7c5d6066bb0c98c33ac25c063262ca191ca33703461d719d6d56f8a04" :answer/id #uuid "d635be7f-0a9d-5943-b972-b869c08b50d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "df82d9ab-6205-5f2a-a674-dfb23d1ad195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106526/selection/d6390209006219fa20a1dcce44e45fad1af081f229774a713e8b61b52cdcb868" :answer-field/choices ["q-106526/selection/51360456424422c7319ad71215d3cd2a01be2d133af9b58170a8436c1ffbdb7c" "q-106526/selection/3c3a2422729e8f6e4970d71ae012fd4cb1e2a604de9d671c1687bee806aa3a17" "q-106526/selection/d6390209006219fa20a1dcce44e45fad1af081f229774a713e8b61b52cdcb868" "q-106526/selection/cb3b7e94a0466971bf58267087e6595d91f5f4d83c446828ecbb1a3af7d0640e" "q-106526/selection/de3475c7c5d6066bb0c98c33ac25c063262ca191ca33703461d719d6d56f8a04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106526" :question/id #uuid "d7d4ecc2-8bf0-5598-b6c5-b9b1ccfe056d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106526" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325938/field-1/45826982e4b507390312e217c8395091e35fc55094a3906dad53c4187480933d" :answer/id #uuid "d1ac7f6f-8694-5a63-a579-010534e0d10e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{16}-\\frac{y^2}{49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325938/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7011158-217e-575a-b3d1-258d0960c19f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325938/field-1/45826982e4b507390312e217c8395091e35fc55094a3906dad53c4187480933d" :answer-field/choices ["q-325938/field-1/45826982e4b507390312e217c8395091e35fc55094a3906dad53c4187480933d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325938" :question/id #uuid "6bacdf2c-490b-532d-a769-2a66216dd8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-325938" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325938/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325938"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325932/field-1/19c4d13383e334bb31b6ed4f798627f85dfa5cb6df6a3f1f22f05a91866d33f8" :answer/id #uuid "bdb1cb49-add0-569c-8932-ebbce9358326" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{20}-\\frac{y^2}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325932/field-1/ddefd9ecafe6167c3d728c2af7f623f0381a524ab967663606b1526b2ff86e98" :answer/id #uuid "f4333225-e404-593e-8291-3d456cc5ed09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{10}-\\frac{y^2}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325932/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "21038133-0473-50e5-bcdb-5d60613e5317" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325932/field-1/19c4d13383e334bb31b6ed4f798627f85dfa5cb6df6a3f1f22f05a91866d33f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-325932/field-1/19c4d13383e334bb31b6ed4f798627f85dfa5cb6df6a3f1f22f05a91866d33f8" "q-325932/field-1/ddefd9ecafe6167c3d728c2af7f623f0381a524ab967663606b1526b2ff86e98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325932" :question/id #uuid "dffd576b-d9e4-51fa-bb28-71ffbae1e4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-325932" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325932/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325932"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325931/field-1/cddb8998dd9fc57d533a2cdc611079b1e26a807aea6a382c87318be7c67b32cb" :answer/id #uuid "2c86ca89-9d51-55f0-9d9b-7e9a1ca21565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{3}-\\frac{y^2}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325931/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "053fcdb4-6606-5e8e-aa29-55d3ec2903d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325931/field-1/cddb8998dd9fc57d533a2cdc611079b1e26a807aea6a382c87318be7c67b32cb" :answer-field/choices ["q-325931/field-1/cddb8998dd9fc57d533a2cdc611079b1e26a807aea6a382c87318be7c67b32cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325931" :question/id #uuid "ce6563f1-55ba-5c31-9507-ab4cb56ae82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-325931" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325931/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325931"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-325985"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077122/assets/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that 